{
	// The creative inventory tab with all of the mod's blocks and items. The game looks the name up in its own domain.
	"game:tabname-vintagecolonies": "Vintage Colonies",
	// Survival Handbook guide pages (config/handbook/vintagecolonies-*.json).
	"handbook-vc-start-title": "Vintage Colonies: 1 - Getting Started",
	"handbook-vc-start-text": "Vintage Colonies lets you found a village of settlers who live, eat, sleep and work for you, like MineColonies. You place a Town Hall, order buildings with the Builder's Wand, and settlers build them and take jobs in them.<br><br><strong>Step 1: get the starting items.</strong> Craft a <strong>Supply Camp</strong> (3 firewood over 2 sticks) and place it. The first camp you place in a world gives you a Town Hall, a Builder's Hut, a Builder's Wand and a Colony Ledger, and the camp's own storage (right-click it) holds some starting materials. If you'd rather craft them: a Builder's Wand is flint on a stick, and a Town Hall is 8 planks around a Builder's Wand.<br><br><strong>Step 2: found the colony.</strong> Hold the Builder's Wand, keep the Town Hall block in your inventory, and right-click the ground. Pick <strong>Town Hall</strong>, move the outline where you want it, and press <strong>Accept</strong>. This claims the land around it and 3 to 4 settlers arrive.<br><br><strong>Step 3: the Builder's Hut.</strong> Place the Builder's Hut the same way, then right-click the hut block and order level 1. Open the Town Hall (right-click it), go to <strong>Citizens</strong> and give a settler the <strong>Builder</strong> job. Put building materials and tools (axe, shovel, pickaxe) in a Warehouse or the Builder's Hut storage.<br><br><strong>Step 4: grow.</strong> Keep food and beds near the Town Hall, add a Warehouse so couriers can move things around, then add work huts. The pages below explain each part.<br><br>The <strong>quest book</strong> (L key, or right-click the Colony Ledger) guides you through all of this step by step.",
	"handbook-vc-settlers-title": "Vintage Colonies: 2 - Settlers",
	"handbook-vc-settlers-text": "<strong>Food.</strong> Settlers get hungry and take food from chests, shelves and crocks inside the colony. Keep bread, fruit, stews or pies near the Town Hall. Without food they slowly starve. A Cook's Hut makes meals for them.<br><br><strong>Healing.</strong> Badly hurt settlers go home to bed until they are well. Each carries a bandage or poultice to use when hurt, and makes a horsetail poultice if there is none. A Healer makes bandages and treats settlers in bed.<br><br><strong>Beds.</strong> At night each settler claims a free bed inside the colony. Without one they sleep on the floor by the Town Hall. A <strong>Residence</strong> gives families a home, and couples need free beds to have children.<br><br><strong>Jobs.</strong> Open the Town Hall, go to <strong>Citizens</strong> and pick a job for each settler. Most jobs need their hut built first. Settlers without a job look after their home and help couriers carry things.<br><br><strong>Skills and traits.</strong> Settlers get better at their job as they work, from Unschooled (0) to Legend of the realm (20). Each level makes the work faster, and levels 5, 10, 15 and 20 give a perk. Upgrade a hut to let them use more of their skill. Skills they have a passion for (+ or ++) grow faster, and some traits mean a settler can never do a job. Each settler has traits, good and bad. See them in the settler's window: right-click a settler.<br><br><strong>Happiness.</strong> Every settler has a mood from 0 to 100. Cooked meals, a varied diet, a bed in a Residence, a partner and work they love make them happier; hunger, no bed and deaths make them sad. Happy settlers work and learn faster. Very unhappy ones down tools for a few hours. See their thoughts on the Stats tab of their window.<br><br><strong>More settlers.</strong> Build a <strong>Tavern</strong>: every day a few people stop by, and you hire them with rusty gears on its Recruits tab. Wanderers also turn up at the colony now and then; welcome them on the Town Hall's Citizens tab.<br><br><strong>Priorities.</strong> The Builder's Hut and every hut that makes more than one thing have a Priorities tab. Use ^ and v, or Do next, to change what they do first.<br><br><strong>Traders.</strong> When a settler comes near a trader and sees it, the trader joins the colony. They get a job that fits their goods, and are already good at it. Some of their goods go to the warehouse. If the colony is full they wait at the Tavern until there is room. A builder then takes their old house down by itself. Clutter in it is destroyed, so pick up anything you want with glue first.<br><br><strong>Clothes.</strong> Clothes wear out. Settlers in rags or cold are a little unhappy, and change into better clothes from the warehouse. A Tailor mends the old ones. Jewellery from a Jeweller makes them happier.<br><br><strong>Smiths.</strong> The Blacksmith makes tools. An Armourer makes and mends metal armor, and a Weaponsmith makes weapons. Until you build those two, the Blacksmith makes their things.<br><br><strong>Giving and taking items.</strong> Right-click a settler and use the Inventory tab, just like your own inventory.",
	"handbook-vc-building-title": "Vintage Colonies: 3 - Building with the Builder's Wand",
	"handbook-vc-building-text": "Huts, the Town Hall and the Warehouse are not placed by hand. Hold the <strong>Builder's Wand</strong>, keep the building's block in your inventory, and right-click the ground. Pick the building from the list (sorted into tabs such as Fundamentals, Agriculture, Craftsmanship, Mining and Military). A see-through ghost of the building shows where it goes: red marks blocks the builder will clear. Turn and move it with the buttons, then press <strong>Accept</strong>.<br><br><strong>Ordering a level.</strong> Right-click the hut block to open its window and order the next level. The builder takes work orders one at a time. The Builder's Hut window shows every order and, under <strong>Items needed</strong>, what is still missing.<br><br><strong>Materials.</strong> The builder takes blocks from the Warehouse and its own hut storage, and couriers bring what it asks for. It needs a shovel, an axe and a pickaxe to clear the ground.<br><br><strong>Asset packs.</strong> The wand asks which style of buildings you want the first time you use it. Change it later on the wand's <strong>Change pack</strong> tab.<br><br><strong>Land.</strong> Every finished hut grows the colony's land around it, and so does each Town Hall level.",
	"handbook-vc-huts-title": "Vintage Colonies: 4 - The Huts",
	"handbook-vc-huts-text": "Every hut is crafted with planks around the outside and two of its own item in the middle column. Place it with the Builder's Wand, order level 1, then give a settler the job in the Town Hall. Sneak and right-click a hut block to open its storage.<br><br><strong>Builder's Hut</strong> (rope): builds and upgrades everything.<br><strong>Warehouse</strong> (chests): the colony's main store. Couriers work from it.<br><strong>Tavern</strong> (rusty gears): hire new settlers.<br><strong>Residence</strong> (dry grass): homes and beds for families.<br><strong>Farmer's Hut</strong> (seeds): the farmer tills, sows and harvests the fields you mark with a <strong>Field marker</strong> (the scarecrow). Needs a hoe.<br><strong>Forester's Hut</strong> (firewood): fells and replants trees. Needs an axe.<br><strong>Potter's Hut</strong> (clay): shapes clay and fires it in pit kilns.<br><strong>Cook's Hut</strong> (bread): cooks meals for the colony.<br><strong>Prospector's Hut</strong> (stones): finds ore and marks it on the map. Needs a prospecting pick.<br><strong>Miner's Hut</strong> (torches): digs a mine and brings up ore. Needs a pickaxe.<br><strong>Quarry</strong> (rock blocks): digs a pit for stone. Needs a pickaxe.<br><strong>Smelter's Hut</strong> (charcoal): melts nuggets into ingots with a fire pit, crucible and ingot molds.<br><strong>Blacksmith's Hut</strong> (ingots): makes tools at a forge and an anvil. Needs a hammer.<br><strong>Sawmill</strong> (logs): saws logs into planks and other wooden things. Needs a saw.<br><strong>Primitive Craftsman</strong> (flint): knaps stone tools for the colony.<br><strong>Guard Tower</strong> (spears) and <strong>Barracks</strong> (bows): homes for guards.<br><br>Each hut window has a <strong>?</strong> button with a guide for that hut.",
	"handbook-vc-storage-title": "Vintage Colonies: 5 - Storage, Couriers and Food",
	"handbook-vc-storage-text": "<strong>Warehouse.</strong> Chests within 5 blocks of the Warehouse block become the colony's store. Workers take what they need from it, and settlers drop off what they collect.<br><br><strong>Couriers.</strong> Give settlers the <strong>Courier</strong> job. They carry what huts ask for from the Warehouse, and take finished goods from the huts back to it.<br><br><strong>Hut storage.</strong> Each hut has its own storage (sneak and right-click the hut block). Chests right next to a hut add more room. Put a worker's tool here to get them started.<br><br><strong>Courier drop-off.</strong> Place a drop-off (a stick over a plank) touching a chest, and couriers empty that chest into the Warehouse. Handy for unloading what you bring home.<br><br><strong>Food.</strong> Keep food in chests inside the colony. A Cook's Hut keeps meals in stock, and its Kitchen tab says how many of each to keep.",
	"handbook-vc-guards-title": "Vintage Colonies: 6 - Guards and Defence",
	"handbook-vc-guards-text": "Give settlers the <strong>Guard</strong> job. A guard with a place at a <strong>Guard Tower</strong> (one guard per level) or a <strong>Barracks</strong> (two per level, and they watch farther) keeps watch at night and sleeps by day. Guards without a tower stay near the Town Hall or escort workers whose hut is set to \"Escort by guard\".<br><br><strong>Tasks.</strong> On the tower's <strong>Settings</strong> tab choose <strong>Guard</strong> (stay at one spot), <strong>Patrol</strong> (walk from hut to hut) or <strong>Follow</strong> (be your bodyguard). Get a <strong>Guard Scepter</strong> from the tower window to set the guard spot or your own patrol route.<br><br><strong>Gear.</strong> Put weapons and armor in the tower's storage or the Warehouse. Guards take the best they can find.<br><br><strong>Alarms.</strong> When a monster hurts a settler inside the colony, guards come running. The <strong>Hostiles</strong> tab picks which creatures they fight.",
	"handbook-vc-tips-title": "Vintage Colonies: 7 - Tips and Commands",
	"handbook-vc-tips-text": "<strong>Something stuck?</strong> Use <strong>Recall</strong> on the Town Hall's Citizens tab to bring a settler back to the Town Hall.<br><br><strong>What is a worker doing?</strong> Open its hut: the Main tab says what it is doing or what it is waiting for, for example \"needs a saw\".<br><br><strong>Commands</strong><br>/colony info: shows the colony you stand in<br>/colony citizens: every settler with their food and bed<br>/colony invite [player], then that player types /colony join<br>/colony leave: leave the colony you stand in<br><br><strong>Settings.</strong> The Town Hall's <strong>Settings</strong> tab has Permadeath (whether dead settlers come back), wanderers and the Warehouse. More options are in ModConfig/createacolony.json.<br><br>Breaking the Town Hall disbands the colony and removes its land claim.",
